\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{First theorem and counterexample lanes}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{research-programme}

\p{The first theorem drafts should remain finite and conditional. The
definitions above support the following questions.}

\ol{
  \li{Under a declared support and verifier-error model, how many independent
  rollouts are required to observe a successful trajectory with stated
  confidence?}
  \li{How do false positives and false negatives bias a policy-gradient or
  group-relative update?}
  \li{Under bounded ratios and controlled advantage variance, how many replay
  updates can be accepted before off-policy error exceeds a tolerance?}
  \li{Can two environments produce the same observed checkpoint history and
  require different future informative feedback, defeating every history-only
  extrapolator on one of them?}
  \li{How should a fixed lifecycle budget be divided when rollout generation,
  verification, and updates have different diminishing returns?}
}

\p{Counterexamples are equally important. Global entropy need not determine
successful support. A large head gradient need not certify policy divergence.
Low-rank checkpoint motion need not imply linear future motion. A higher
training reward need not improve independent utility.}

\p{These are FTIP research directions. The conditional gradient inequality and
empirical gate come from \citef{miao2026when}. The trajectory-compression
results in \citef{chen2026lowrank} are empirical. Their claims and assumptions
should remain separate when the formal statements are written.}
